<!--
    ddd_gui_resources.qrc

    The application's graphics, compiled into the binary
    Domesday Duplicator - LaserDisc RF sampler
    SPDX-FileCopyrightText: 2026 Simon Inns
    SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-or-later

    Component-local copies of the repository's graphics, not references to
    them (AGENTS.md §2), so the application's artwork travels with the
    component that ships it.
-->
<RCC>
    <qresource prefix="/graphics">
        <file>logo-250.png</file>
        <file>logo-150.png</file>
        <file>icon-16.png</file>
        <file>icon-24.png</file>
        <file>icon-32.png</file>
        <file>icon-48.png</file>
        <file>icon-64.png</file>
        <file>icon-128.png</file>
        <file>icon-256.png</file>
    </qresource>

    <!--
        The bring-up wizard's photographs.

        The same three images the "Bringing up a new or legacy board" and
        hardware-programming documentation pages carry, downscaled here to what
        the wizard actually displays: three 550 KB originals would be a real
        addition to every Flatpak, MSI and DMG for pixels nobody sees. The
        full-resolution copies live beside the docs pages that show them, and
        both copies enter the tree together so the wizard and the page cannot
        drift apart — which is the whole reason for using photographs rather
        than prose about pin positions.

        PNG, not the JPEG the docs copies use, because Qt decodes PNG in QtGui
        itself and decodes JPEG only through an image-format plugin. A plugin
        is a file a packaging step can fail to ship, and when it does the
        failure is silent: QPixmap returns nothing, the wizard shows an empty
        box, and the application is otherwise perfectly well. That happened on
        Windows. A format that cannot go missing is worth more here than the
        smaller file, so these are quantised to a 256-colour palette to pay for
        it — indistinguishable at the size the wizard draws them.

        Named for what the jumper *is* rather than for how it looks: the
        firmware README, the documentation and the wizard all say fitted and
        removed, so a user never has to translate between two vocabularies for
        one jumper.
    -->
    <qresource prefix="/photographs">
        <file>fx3-j4-fitted.png</file>
        <file>fx3-j4-removed.png</file>
        <file>fpga-usb-port.png</file>
    </qresource>
</RCC>
